1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,172
I read the same eighty megabytes two ways.

2
00:00:03,172 --> 00:00:04,102
As one file,

3
00:00:04,102 --> 00:00:06,426
it took five hundredths of a second.

4
00:00:06,426 --> 00:00:08,460
As twenty thousand small files,

5
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:10,552
it took one point three seconds.

6
00:00:10,552 --> 00:00:12,237
Twenty-five times slower,

7
00:00:12,237 --> 00:00:13,748
on a plain local disk.

8
00:00:14,098 --> 00:00:15,693
On a cloud object store,

9
00:00:15,693 --> 00:00:17,744
where every request has a delay,

10
00:00:17,744 --> 00:00:20,820
the same mistake would be hundreds of times worse.

11
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:23,383
This lesson is about where AI data lives,

12
00:00:23,383 --> 00:00:24,751
how to read it fast,

13
00:00:24,751 --> 00:00:27,257
and how vectors are stored and searched.

14
00:00:28,507 --> 00:00:31,908
Welcome to lesson four of the cloud for A I engineers course,

15
00:00:31,908 --> 00:00:33,216
on The Code Notebook.

16
00:00:33,566 --> 00:00:36,700
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

17
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:37,622
the description.

18
00:00:38,872 --> 00:00:41,954
So far we have rented chips and packed containers.

19
00:00:41,954 --> 00:00:45,550
But a GPU with nothing to read is just an expensive heater.

20
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:47,605
Lesson four is about feeding it.

21
00:00:47,605 --> 00:00:49,032
Where does data live,

22
00:00:49,032 --> 00:00:50,630
how do you read it fast,

23
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:52,513
how do you save a checkpoint,

24
00:00:52,513 --> 00:00:54,112
and how do vectors work?

25
00:00:54,462 --> 00:00:56,729
I mix measurements and models here.

26
00:00:56,729 --> 00:01:00,625
The file reads and the vector tests are real measurements on my

27
00:01:00,625 --> 00:01:02,718
machine, with synthetic vectors.

28
00:01:02,718 --> 00:01:06,556
The cloud object store timing is a model with assumed numbers,

29
00:01:06,556 --> 00:01:08,126
and I say so on screen.

30
00:01:09,376 --> 00:01:11,978
Start with the main place AI data lives,

31
00:01:11,978 --> 00:01:13,102
object storage.

32
00:01:13,102 --> 00:01:14,639
Services like S three,

33
00:01:14,639 --> 00:01:17,005
Google Cloud Storage and Azure Blob.

34
00:01:17,005 --> 00:01:20,790
Think of a giant filing cabinet where every file has a name,

35
00:01:20,790 --> 00:01:23,569
and you can store almost unlimited amounts,

36
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:24,574
very cheaply,

37
00:01:24,574 --> 00:01:26,112
with great durability.

38
00:01:26,462 --> 00:01:28,205
The catch is how you reach in.

39
00:01:28,205 --> 00:01:30,102
You ask for a whole file by name.

40
00:01:30,102 --> 00:01:31,999
You do not edit the middle of it.

41
00:01:31,999 --> 00:01:34,870
That one fact shapes everything else in this lesson.

42
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:37,996
Cloud storage comes in tiers.

43
00:01:37,996 --> 00:01:39,928
The standard tier is regional.

44
00:01:39,928 --> 00:01:42,713
Your data is copied across several buildings,

45
00:01:42,713 --> 00:01:44,191
so it survives a fire.

46
00:01:44,191 --> 00:01:45,441
That is the truth,

47
00:01:45,441 --> 00:01:47,317
the copy you must never lose.

48
00:01:47,667 --> 00:01:48,516
Fast tiers,

49
00:01:48,516 --> 00:01:51,575
such as S three Express or Google's Rapid buckets,

50
00:01:51,575 --> 00:01:53,048
live in a single zone,

51
00:01:53,048 --> 00:01:54,634
right next to your GPUs.

52
00:01:54,634 --> 00:01:56,107
They are much quicker,

53
00:01:56,107 --> 00:01:57,579
but one building only.

54
00:01:57,929 --> 00:02:01,708
And archive tiers are cheap for data you almost never read.

55
00:02:01,708 --> 00:02:03,868
The rule is region equals truth,

56
00:02:03,868 --> 00:02:05,187
zone equals speed.

57
00:02:05,187 --> 00:02:08,007
Never keep your only copy in the fast tier.

58
00:02:09,257 --> 00:02:10,570
So what goes where?

59
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:11,769
Datasets, shards,

60
00:02:11,769 --> 00:02:16,167
final checkpoints and model weights belong in the regional standard tier.

61
00:02:16,167 --> 00:02:19,879
That is about two point three cents per gigabyte per month at

62
00:02:19,879 --> 00:02:21,421
the S three list price.

63
00:02:21,771 --> 00:02:22,692
Hot shards,

64
00:02:22,692 --> 00:02:26,928
scratch space and the most recent checkpoints can sit in the fast

65
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:27,849
zonal tier,

66
00:02:27,849 --> 00:02:30,181
as long as a regional copy exists.

67
00:02:30,181 --> 00:02:34,171
Old checkpoints and raw data you rarely touch go to cooler or

68
00:02:34,171 --> 00:02:35,276
archive tiers.

69
00:02:36,526 --> 00:02:38,865
Here is the surprise on cloud bills.

70
00:02:38,865 --> 00:02:40,385
Storing data is cheap.

71
00:02:40,385 --> 00:02:41,846
Moving it out is not.

72
00:02:41,846 --> 00:02:43,775
That charge is called egress.

73
00:02:43,775 --> 00:02:44,886
At list prices,

74
00:02:44,886 --> 00:02:48,920
the big clouds charge around nine to twelve cents per gigabyte to

75
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:50,674
send data to the internet.

76
00:02:51,024 --> 00:02:54,712
Moving data between regions or between zones also costs.

77
00:02:54,712 --> 00:02:57,910
So before you chase cheap GPUs in another cloud,

78
00:02:57,910 --> 00:03:00,738
budget the cost of moving your data there.

79
00:03:00,738 --> 00:03:01,783
One neocloud,

80
00:03:01,783 --> 00:03:03,074
Cloudflare R two,

81
00:03:03,074 --> 00:03:04,980
charges nothing for egress,

82
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:08,669
which is why people use it to serve data to many clouds.

83
00:03:09,919 --> 00:03:11,201
Let us do the sum.

84
00:03:11,201 --> 00:03:14,696
One hundred terabytes is one hundred thousand gigabytes.

85
00:03:14,696 --> 00:03:18,308
At the nine cent list price that is nine thousand dollars.

86
00:03:18,308 --> 00:03:20,638
Volume tiers bring it down somewhat,

87
00:03:20,638 --> 00:03:23,842
but the order of magnitude is thousands of dollars.

88
00:03:24,192 --> 00:03:27,838
That is why you should design so data stays near the compute.

89
00:03:27,838 --> 00:03:30,867
It is cheaper to move the job to the data than the

90
00:03:30,867 --> 00:03:31,988
data to the job.

91
00:03:31,988 --> 00:03:34,232
This is arithmetic from list prices,

92
00:03:34,232 --> 00:03:35,129
not a quote.

93
00:03:36,379 --> 00:03:38,583
Now back to my opening mistake.

94
00:03:38,583 --> 00:03:43,367
Object stores are brilliant at big sequential reads and terrible at many

95
00:03:43,367 --> 00:03:44,248
tiny ones.

96
00:03:44,248 --> 00:03:48,025
Every request has a fixed delay before any data arrives.

97
00:03:48,025 --> 00:03:52,369
Fetch ten thousand tiny files and you pay that delay ten thousand

98
00:03:52,369 --> 00:03:52,999
times.

99
00:03:53,349 --> 00:03:56,962
This is the number one beginner mistake with training data.

100
00:03:56,962 --> 00:03:59,370
Millions of small image or text files,

101
00:03:59,370 --> 00:04:00,517
read one by one.

102
00:04:00,517 --> 00:04:02,353
The cure is called sharding.

103
00:04:03,603 --> 00:04:05,345
The experiment is simple.

104
00:04:05,345 --> 00:04:08,591
I made eighty megabytes of random data three ways.

105
00:04:08,591 --> 00:04:11,235
Twenty thousand files of four kilobytes.

106
00:04:11,235 --> 00:04:13,218
Eighty files of one megabyte.

107
00:04:13,218 --> 00:04:15,502
And a single eighty megabyte file,

108
00:04:15,502 --> 00:04:17,004
which I call a shard.

109
00:04:17,354 --> 00:04:21,149
I dropped the operating system's file cache before every read,

110
00:04:21,149 --> 00:04:23,563
so I measured the disk and not memory.

111
00:04:23,563 --> 00:04:24,943
Best of three tries.

112
00:04:24,943 --> 00:04:28,565
This part is a real measurement on my machine's local disk.

113
00:04:29,815 --> 00:04:33,690
Twenty thousand small files took one point three four seconds,

114
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:35,863
about sixty megabytes per second.

115
00:04:35,863 --> 00:04:38,798
Eighty one megabyte files took point oh eight.

116
00:04:38,798 --> 00:04:41,147
The single shard took point oh five,

117
00:04:41,147 --> 00:04:43,906
about fifteen hundred megabytes per second.

118
00:04:44,256 --> 00:04:47,674
That is twenty-five times slower for the tiny files,

119
00:04:47,674 --> 00:04:49,749
reading exactly the same data.

120
00:04:49,749 --> 00:04:51,823
And this is a fast local disk.

121
00:04:51,823 --> 00:04:54,813
Cloud storage has far more delay per request.

122
00:04:56,063 --> 00:04:58,420
Now a model of a cloud object store.

123
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:01,071
I assume twenty milliseconds per request,

124
00:05:01,071 --> 00:05:04,017
and a hundred megabytes per second per stream.

125
00:05:04,017 --> 00:05:05,725
Those are my assumptions,

126
00:05:05,725 --> 00:05:07,316
and real services vary.

127
00:05:07,666 --> 00:05:11,240
Twenty thousand small files would take four hundred seconds.

128
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:12,190
Eighty files,

129
00:05:12,190 --> 00:05:13,698
two point four seconds.

130
00:05:13,698 --> 00:05:14,479
One shard,

131
00:05:14,479 --> 00:05:15,373
point eight.

132
00:05:15,373 --> 00:05:16,155
The delay,

133
00:05:16,155 --> 00:05:17,104
not the data,

134
00:05:17,104 --> 00:05:17,886
dominates.

135
00:05:19,136 --> 00:05:21,272
So pack your samples into shards.

136
00:05:21,272 --> 00:05:22,773
A shard is a big file,

137
00:05:22,773 --> 00:05:25,602
from about a hundred megabytes to a gigabyte,

138
00:05:25,602 --> 00:05:28,084
holding many samples one after another.

139
00:05:28,084 --> 00:05:31,028
Each training worker holds one shard at a time,

140
00:05:31,028 --> 00:05:32,875
plus a small shuffle buffer.

141
00:05:33,225 --> 00:05:34,695
Two popular formats.

142
00:05:34,695 --> 00:05:36,900
WebDataset uses plain tar files,

143
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:40,084
one sample being a few files with the same name.

144
00:05:40,084 --> 00:05:43,637
Mosaic's streaming format reads shards from any cloud,

145
00:05:43,637 --> 00:05:46,148
can resume in the middle of an epoch,

146
00:05:46,148 --> 00:05:50,312
and gives the same sample order whatever number of GPUs you use.

147
00:05:51,562 --> 00:05:52,905
A reassuring fact.

148
00:05:52,905 --> 00:05:56,139
Plain text training is rarely limited by storage.

149
00:05:56,139 --> 00:05:57,847
Fifteen trillion tokens,

150
00:05:57,847 --> 00:05:59,495
stored as two byte ids,

151
00:05:59,495 --> 00:06:01,325
is about thirty terabytes,

152
00:06:01,325 --> 00:06:03,217
read roughly once per pass.

153
00:06:03,217 --> 00:06:04,620
That is manageable.

154
00:06:04,970 --> 00:06:07,854
Storage gets stressed by pictures and video,

155
00:06:07,854 --> 00:06:10,617
by small random reads you cannot re shard,

156
00:06:10,617 --> 00:06:12,359
and by checkpoint bursts.

157
00:06:12,359 --> 00:06:13,440
Profile first,

158
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:17,224
and only buy fast storage when the GPUs are really waiting.

159
00:06:18,474 --> 00:06:20,068
When you do need speed,

160
00:06:20,068 --> 00:06:23,018
the standard answer is a parallel file system,

161
00:06:23,018 --> 00:06:24,139
usually Lustre,

162
00:06:24,139 --> 00:06:27,208
offered as a managed service on every big cloud.

163
00:06:27,208 --> 00:06:29,922
Neoclouds use products like WEKA and VAST.

164
00:06:29,922 --> 00:06:32,814
They look like an ordinary disk to your code.

165
00:06:33,164 --> 00:06:34,905
There is a pricing trick.

166
00:06:34,905 --> 00:06:37,366
You buy bandwidth by buying capacity.

167
00:06:37,366 --> 00:06:41,267
Small data and a hungry job means paying for empty terabytes.

168
00:06:41,267 --> 00:06:42,408
The pattern is:

169
00:06:42,408 --> 00:06:44,149
the bucket is the master,

170
00:06:44,149 --> 00:06:47,630
the fast file system is a temporary copy for this job,

171
00:06:47,630 --> 00:06:49,311
torn down after the run.

172
00:06:50,561 --> 00:06:53,237
Checkpoints are the other big storage event.

173
00:06:53,237 --> 00:06:56,581
A full training checkpoint stores more than the weights.

174
00:06:56,581 --> 00:06:59,870
In the common setup it is fourteen bytes per parameter.

175
00:06:59,870 --> 00:07:01,208
Two for the weights,

176
00:07:01,208 --> 00:07:03,159
four for a full precision copy,

177
00:07:03,159 --> 00:07:06,002
and four each for the optimiser's two memories.

178
00:07:06,352 --> 00:07:10,301
So a seventy billion parameter model has a checkpoint close to one

179
00:07:10,301 --> 00:07:13,234
terabyte. A four hundred and five billion model,

180
00:07:13,234 --> 00:07:14,757
about five point seven.

181
00:07:14,757 --> 00:07:17,634
That is arithmetic from the notebook's formula.

182
00:07:18,884 --> 00:07:21,002
If training waits for the write,

183
00:07:21,002 --> 00:07:22,061
the GPUs idle.

184
00:07:22,061 --> 00:07:26,120
A terabyte written at ten gigabytes per second is about a hundred

185
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:28,885
seconds. At a hundred gigabytes per second,

186
00:07:28,885 --> 00:07:31,297
ten. These speeds are my assumptions.

187
00:07:31,647 --> 00:07:33,285
The big models are worse.

188
00:07:33,285 --> 00:07:35,261
The fix is asynchronous saving.

189
00:07:35,261 --> 00:07:37,689
Copy the state to memory or local disk,

190
00:07:37,689 --> 00:07:39,158
let training continue,

191
00:07:39,158 --> 00:07:41,925
and drain it to the bucket in the background.

192
00:07:43,175 --> 00:07:44,564
Now structured data,

193
00:07:44,564 --> 00:07:46,242
like tables of customers,

194
00:07:46,242 --> 00:07:47,457
tickets and logs.

195
00:07:47,457 --> 00:07:50,062
The modern pattern is called a lakehouse.

196
00:07:50,062 --> 00:07:52,724
Files in a columnar format called Parquet,

197
00:07:52,724 --> 00:07:54,460
sitting in object storage.

198
00:07:54,810 --> 00:07:55,483
On top,

199
00:07:55,483 --> 00:08:00,130
a table format such as Delta or Iceberg adds transactions and snapshots.

200
00:08:00,130 --> 00:08:01,292
On top of that,

201
00:08:01,292 --> 00:08:02,881
a catalog holds names,

202
00:08:02,881 --> 00:08:04,594
permissions and lineage.

203
00:08:04,594 --> 00:08:06,611
Any query engine can read it.

204
00:08:06,611 --> 00:08:08,079
Engines come and go,

205
00:08:08,079 --> 00:08:09,302
the tables stay.

206
00:08:10,552 --> 00:08:13,857
If you cannot reproduce last month's training data,

207
00:08:13,857 --> 00:08:16,501
you cannot reproduce last month's model.

208
00:08:16,501 --> 00:08:18,784
Version your data at three levels.

209
00:08:18,784 --> 00:08:21,609
Files, with tools like DVC or Hugging Face.

210
00:08:21,609 --> 00:08:22,570
Whole lakes,

211
00:08:22,570 --> 00:08:23,652
with branches,

212
00:08:23,652 --> 00:08:24,673
using lakeFS.

213
00:08:24,673 --> 00:08:25,575
And tables,

214
00:08:25,575 --> 00:08:26,777
using snapshots.

215
00:08:27,127 --> 00:08:29,289
Pin every training run to a version.

216
00:08:29,289 --> 00:08:30,533
In the table world,

217
00:08:30,533 --> 00:08:33,398
that means a snapshot number you can query later.

218
00:08:34,648 --> 00:08:36,444
A trap with Delta tables.

219
00:08:36,444 --> 00:08:38,673
Time travel needs the old files.

220
00:08:38,673 --> 00:08:39,602
By default,

221
00:08:39,602 --> 00:08:43,131
the clean up job deletes files older than seven days.

222
00:08:43,131 --> 00:08:46,475
So last month's training snapshot is already gone.

223
00:08:46,825 --> 00:08:49,764
Set the retention to at least as long as you want to

224
00:08:49,764 --> 00:08:50,499
look back,

225
00:08:50,499 --> 00:08:51,864
ninety days is common.

226
00:08:51,864 --> 00:08:53,596
This is a documented default,

227
00:08:53,596 --> 00:08:55,118
not something I measured.

228
00:08:56,368 --> 00:08:59,334
A reproducible training run records four things.

229
00:08:59,334 --> 00:09:00,589
The data snapshot,

230
00:09:00,589 --> 00:09:01,729
the code commit,

231
00:09:01,729 --> 00:09:04,239
the tokenizer and preprocessing version,

232
00:09:04,239 --> 00:09:06,292
and the embedding model version.

233
00:09:06,292 --> 00:09:08,346
Put them in your model registry.

234
00:09:08,696 --> 00:09:12,724
And before data is written into training sets or search indexes,

235
00:09:12,724 --> 00:09:13,494
check it.

236
00:09:13,494 --> 00:09:14,560
Schema, nulls,

237
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:16,337
duplicates, personal data,

238
00:09:16,337 --> 00:09:18,766
and overlap with your evaluation set.

239
00:09:18,766 --> 00:09:21,491
Bad data costs more the later you find it.

240
00:09:22,741 --> 00:09:23,636
Now vectors.

241
00:09:23,636 --> 00:09:25,539
An embedding model turns text,

242
00:09:25,539 --> 00:09:26,434
or an image,

243
00:09:26,434 --> 00:09:27,945
into a list of numbers,

244
00:09:27,945 --> 00:09:29,065
called a vector.

245
00:09:29,065 --> 00:09:31,359
Similar meanings land close together.

246
00:09:31,359 --> 00:09:32,142
To search,

247
00:09:32,142 --> 00:09:35,276
you embed the question and find the nearest vectors.

248
00:09:35,626 --> 00:09:39,611
The simplest search compares the question to every stored vector.

249
00:09:39,611 --> 00:09:40,650
That is exact,

250
00:09:40,650 --> 00:09:43,306
but it slows down as the collection grows.

251
00:09:43,306 --> 00:09:45,789
Real systems use an approximate method,

252
00:09:45,789 --> 00:09:48,618
trading a little accuracy for a lot of speed.

253
00:09:49,868 --> 00:09:51,938
How much memory do vectors need?

254
00:09:51,938 --> 00:09:55,964
Bytes per vector equals the dimensions times the bytes per number.

255
00:09:55,964 --> 00:10:00,564
A model with seven hundred and sixty-eight dimensions in the standard format

256
00:10:00,564 --> 00:10:03,842
uses three thousand and seventy-two bytes per vector.

257
00:10:04,192 --> 00:10:08,256
Fifty thousand of them is about a hundred and fifty-four megabytes.

258
00:10:08,256 --> 00:10:10,603
Ten million is over thirty gigabytes.

259
00:10:10,603 --> 00:10:13,179
Memory is the main cost of vector search,

260
00:10:13,179 --> 00:10:14,724
so compression matters.

261
00:10:15,974 --> 00:10:17,444
I tested compression.

262
00:10:17,444 --> 00:10:22,323
Fifty thousand synthetic vectors of seven hundred and sixty-eight numbers each.

263
00:10:22,323 --> 00:10:24,027
They are random clusters,

264
00:10:24,027 --> 00:10:25,438
not real embeddings,

265
00:10:25,438 --> 00:10:27,672
so treat the results as a pattern.

266
00:10:27,672 --> 00:10:30,435
Exact search on the full copy is the truth.

267
00:10:30,785 --> 00:10:33,315
Then I stored them as half size numbers,

268
00:10:33,315 --> 00:10:34,694
as one byte numbers,

269
00:10:34,694 --> 00:10:36,017
and as single bits.

270
00:10:36,017 --> 00:10:37,914
And I measured recall at ten,

271
00:10:37,914 --> 00:10:41,709
meaning how many of the true top ten each version still found.

272
00:10:42,959 --> 00:10:44,149
Half size numbers,

273
00:10:44,149 --> 00:10:45,123
float sixteen,

274
00:10:45,123 --> 00:10:46,800
found every true neighbour.

275
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:48,207
Recall one point zero,

276
00:10:48,207 --> 00:10:49,452
at half the memory.

277
00:10:49,452 --> 00:10:53,293
One byte numbers found ninety-eight and a half percent at a quarter

278
00:10:53,293 --> 00:10:54,267
of the memory.

279
00:10:54,617 --> 00:10:57,006
Single bits shrank it thirty-two times,

280
00:10:57,006 --> 00:10:59,840
but on their own found only twenty-one percent.

281
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:03,729
After rescoring the best hundred candidates with the full numbers,

282
00:11:03,729 --> 00:11:06,229
recall came back to seventy-four percent.

283
00:11:06,579 --> 00:11:08,255
So bits are a first filter,

284
00:11:08,255 --> 00:11:09,228
not an answer.

285
00:11:09,228 --> 00:11:13,282
The notebook warns they work worse under about four hundred dimensions,

286
00:11:13,282 --> 00:11:15,120
and better as dimensions grow.

287
00:11:16,370 --> 00:11:17,766
Now the speed side.

288
00:11:17,766 --> 00:11:20,681
The most popular approximate method is HNSW.

289
00:11:20,681 --> 00:11:22,503
It builds a layered graph.

290
00:11:22,503 --> 00:11:25,175
Imagine a map with motorways at the top,

291
00:11:25,175 --> 00:11:26,936
main roads in the middle,

292
00:11:26,936 --> 00:11:29,061
and every street at the bottom.

293
00:11:29,411 --> 00:11:30,951
A search starts at the top,

294
00:11:30,951 --> 00:11:33,684
hops to whichever node is closest to your question,

295
00:11:33,684 --> 00:11:35,721
and drops a layer when it gets stuck.

296
00:11:36,071 --> 00:11:39,225
Finally it looks around its neighbours on the bottom layer.

297
00:11:39,225 --> 00:11:41,077
It never visits most of the data.

298
00:11:42,327 --> 00:11:43,467
Two knobs matter.

299
00:11:43,467 --> 00:11:44,771
M is how many links,

300
00:11:44,771 --> 00:11:45,586
or friends,

301
00:11:45,586 --> 00:11:46,672
each node keeps.

302
00:11:46,672 --> 00:11:48,573
More friends means more memory.

303
00:11:48,573 --> 00:11:50,311
And ef is how hard you look,

304
00:11:50,311 --> 00:11:52,321
when building and when searching.

305
00:11:52,321 --> 00:11:54,711
Looking harder is slower but finds more.

306
00:11:55,061 --> 00:11:58,676
Memory is about N times four dimensions plus eight M bytes.

307
00:11:58,676 --> 00:12:02,348
For my test that formula said a hundred and sixty megabytes,

308
00:12:02,348 --> 00:12:05,561
and the real index file was a hundred and sixty-one.

309
00:12:05,561 --> 00:12:07,283
Close enough to plan with.

310
00:12:08,533 --> 00:12:10,522
Here is recall against effort.

311
00:12:10,522 --> 00:12:11,575
At effort ten,

312
00:12:11,575 --> 00:12:13,506
recall was fifty-six percent.

313
00:12:13,506 --> 00:12:14,267
At fifty,

314
00:12:14,267 --> 00:12:17,309
ninety-six, in point two milliseconds per query.

315
00:12:17,309 --> 00:12:18,304
At a hundred,

316
00:12:18,304 --> 00:12:20,410
ninety-nine point eight percent.

317
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:25,147
Exact search took one point two one milliseconds for the same questions.

318
00:12:25,147 --> 00:12:27,224
So at ninety-six percent recall,

319
00:12:27,224 --> 00:12:29,590
the graph was about six times faster,

320
00:12:29,590 --> 00:12:32,245
and the gap grows as the collection grows.

321
00:12:33,495 --> 00:12:35,112
HNSW lives in memory.

322
00:12:35,112 --> 00:12:36,857
For bigger collections,

323
00:12:36,857 --> 00:12:38,409
other designs exist.

324
00:12:38,409 --> 00:12:42,805
IVF with compression clusters the data and squeezes each vector.

325
00:12:42,805 --> 00:12:45,520
DiskANN keeps the graph on fast disks.

326
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:49,399
And object tiered systems keep vectors in cheap storage,

327
00:12:49,399 --> 00:12:52,826
with search taking around a hundred milliseconds.

328
00:12:53,176 --> 00:12:54,998
No family wins everywhere.

329
00:12:54,998 --> 00:12:56,821
Memory is fast and costly.

330
00:12:56,821 --> 00:12:59,797
Disk and object storage are slower and cheap.

331
00:12:59,797 --> 00:13:03,745
You choose by how many vectors you have and how quickly users

332
00:13:03,745 --> 00:13:04,900
need an answer.

333
00:13:06,150 --> 00:13:08,052
Which product should you pick?

334
00:13:08,052 --> 00:13:10,066
Start with what you already run.

335
00:13:10,066 --> 00:13:11,465
If you have Postgres,

336
00:13:11,465 --> 00:13:14,543
the pgvector extension handles millions of vectors,

337
00:13:14,543 --> 00:13:16,557
and it stores half size numbers,

338
00:13:16,557 --> 00:13:18,683
which my test showed lose nothing.

339
00:13:18,683 --> 00:13:21,984
Object backed stores such as S three Vectors suit huge,

340
00:13:21,984 --> 00:13:23,663
cheap, slower collections.

341
00:13:24,013 --> 00:13:25,975
Dedicated databases suit heavy,

342
00:13:25,975 --> 00:13:27,433
low latency workloads.

343
00:13:27,433 --> 00:13:30,685
And all of them work better with keyword search added,

344
00:13:30,685 --> 00:13:32,816
which is a topic for lesson eight.

345
00:13:34,066 --> 00:13:35,682
For ShopNest Copilot.

346
00:13:35,682 --> 00:13:40,077
Raw tickets and evaluation sets live in regional object storage,

347
00:13:40,077 --> 00:13:41,435
packed in shards.

348
00:13:41,435 --> 00:13:44,279
The nightly evaluation job reads shards,

349
00:13:44,279 --> 00:13:46,347
not thousands of tiny files.

350
00:13:46,347 --> 00:13:48,997
Checkpoints are saved asynchronously.

351
00:13:49,347 --> 00:13:50,917
The support articles,

352
00:13:50,917 --> 00:13:53,053
a few hundred thousand chunks,

353
00:13:53,053 --> 00:13:56,759
go in pgvector as half size vectors with an HNSW index.

354
00:13:56,759 --> 00:13:59,649
And we record the embedding model version,

355
00:13:59,649 --> 00:14:02,413
so we can rebuild the index at any time.

356
00:14:03,663 --> 00:14:05,578
Four words to carry forward.

357
00:14:05,578 --> 00:14:08,392
Shard: a big file packed with many samples.

358
00:14:08,392 --> 00:14:11,266
Egress: the charge for data leaving a cloud.

359
00:14:11,616 --> 00:14:15,323
Recall: the share of true nearest neighbours a search returns.

360
00:14:15,323 --> 00:14:16,503
And quantisation:

361
00:14:16,503 --> 00:14:19,368
storing numbers with fewer bits to save memory.

362
00:14:20,618 --> 00:14:21,906
Interview corner.

363
00:14:21,906 --> 00:14:25,891
Asked why not store training data as millions of small files,

364
00:14:25,891 --> 00:14:29,201
say: every object store request has a fixed delay,

365
00:14:29,201 --> 00:14:31,776
so small reads are dominated by delay.

366
00:14:31,776 --> 00:14:35,823
Pack samples into shards of a hundred megabytes to a gigabyte.

367
00:14:35,823 --> 00:14:36,742
In my test,

368
00:14:36,742 --> 00:14:39,562
small files read twenty-five times slower.

369
00:14:39,912 --> 00:14:41,845
Asked how to cut vector memory,

370
00:14:41,845 --> 00:14:44,331
say: store half size or one byte numbers.

371
00:14:44,331 --> 00:14:45,159
In my test,

372
00:14:45,159 --> 00:14:49,025
one byte numbers kept recall at ninety-eight and a half percent at

373
00:14:49,025 --> 00:14:50,571
a quarter of the memory,

374
00:14:50,571 --> 00:14:52,836
but plain bits dropped to twenty-one.

375
00:14:54,086 --> 00:14:54,887
Your turn.

376
00:14:54,887 --> 00:14:57,520
The zip for this lesson has three scripts.

377
00:14:57,520 --> 00:15:01,585
The first compares reading small files and shards on your own disk.

378
00:15:01,585 --> 00:15:04,963
The second compares vector formats and the graph index.

379
00:15:04,963 --> 00:15:08,054
The third is the checkpoint and egress arithmetic.

380
00:15:08,404 --> 00:15:10,094
Change the vector dimensions,

381
00:15:10,094 --> 00:15:11,476
or the number of files,

382
00:15:11,476 --> 00:15:12,654
and see what moves.

383
00:15:12,654 --> 00:15:15,727
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

384
00:15:16,977 --> 00:15:18,563
Recap. Region is truth,

385
00:15:18,563 --> 00:15:19,621
zone is speed,

386
00:15:19,621 --> 00:15:21,501
and egress is the real bill.

387
00:15:21,501 --> 00:15:22,971
Small files are slow,

388
00:15:22,971 --> 00:15:24,087
so pack shards.

389
00:15:24,087 --> 00:15:27,907
Twenty thousand files read twenty-five times slower than one.

390
00:15:27,907 --> 00:15:30,786
A checkpoint is fourteen bytes per parameter.

391
00:15:30,786 --> 00:15:32,079
Version your data,

392
00:15:32,079 --> 00:15:34,136
and mind the seven day default.

393
00:15:34,486 --> 00:15:37,899
Half size or one byte vectors keep nearly all recall at a

394
00:15:37,899 --> 00:15:39,913
half or a quarter of the memory.

395
00:15:39,913 --> 00:15:43,382
A graph index found ninety-six percent of true neighbours,

396
00:15:43,382 --> 00:15:45,564
six times faster than exact search.

397
00:15:45,564 --> 00:15:46,459
Next lesson:

398
00:15:46,459 --> 00:15:47,690
training at scale.

399
00:15:47,690 --> 00:15:49,145
Where the memory goes,

400
00:15:49,145 --> 00:15:50,767
and how to split a model.

401
00:15:52,017 --> 00:15:53,251
In the next lesson:

402
00:15:53,251 --> 00:15:54,431
training at scale.

403
00:15:54,431 --> 00:15:55,825
Where the memory goes,

404
00:15:55,825 --> 00:15:58,025
and how to split a model across GPUs.

405
00:15:58,375 --> 00:15:59,967
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

406
00:15:59,967 --> 00:16:01,241
See you in the notebook.

